WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.920 --> 00:00:01.753
- Hi everyone.

00:00:01.753 --> 00:00:03.140
Sal Khan here from Khan Academy.

00:00:03.140 --> 00:00:04.620
Welcome to the Homeroom Livestream.

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:06.390
It's been a while since we did one,

00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:08.840
but we're back and we
have an amazing guest,

00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:10.910
Wendy Kopp, CEO of Teach for All,

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:12.970
also the founder of Teach for America.

00:00:12.970 --> 00:00:14.620
We're gonna talk about her journey

00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:16.690
and all of her learnings about education

00:00:16.690 --> 00:00:17.900
and where we might be going.

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:19.380
But before we do that,

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:21.140
I will give my standard announcements.

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:23.570
First, a reminder that
we are a not-for-profit

00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:24.480
here at Khan Academy.

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:25.720
So if you're in a position to do so,

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:29.040
please think about gonna
khanacademy.org/donate

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:31.020
and making a donation of any size.

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:32.340
It really does make a difference

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:36.220
and it's what allows us to serve millions,

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:39.650
tens of millions of
students around the world.

00:00:39.650 --> 00:00:42.590
The next thing I'd like to
make sure that we recognize

00:00:42.590 --> 00:00:44.225
many folks who have stepped up,

00:00:44.225 --> 00:00:47.400
especially when the pandemic hit

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:50.680
and more folks than ever were
dependent upon Khan Academy.

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:53.940
Special thanks to Bank of
America, AT&amp;T, Google.org,

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:57.500
Novartis, Fastly, and General
Motors for really stepping up.

00:00:57.500 --> 00:00:59.520
And as we all know, unfortunately,

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:02.400
we're still in this pseudo-pandemic mode,

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:05.960
but even post-pandemic
this is where Khan Academy

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:07.400
might be even more important to make sure

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:09.860
that we can get everyone back up to speed

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:11.000
and have a strong foundation,

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:13.000
and having everyone
learn at their own pace.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:14.210
Last but not least,

00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:17.930
there is a version of this
livestream in podcast form,

00:01:17.930 --> 00:01:19.640
wherever you get your podcasts,

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:22.010
Homeroom with Sal, the podcast.

00:01:22.010 --> 00:01:23.770
With that out of the way,

00:01:23.770 --> 00:01:26.010
I'm so excited to introduce Wendy Kopp,

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:27.710
who I consider a good friend

00:01:27.710 --> 00:01:29.790
and someone who I generally look up to

00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:33.180
in all things in my life,

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:37.460
including founding
organizations and all the rest.

00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:39.050
Wendy, welcome.

00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:39.883
- Thank you.

00:01:39.883 --> 00:01:41.380
So good to be here.

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:42.343
Looking forward.

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:45.630
- So, maybe a good, and
I'll remind everyone

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:47.140
who's watching on Facebook or YouTube,

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:49.210
any questions you have for
Wendy about her journey

00:01:49.210 --> 00:01:50.646
or about education generally,

00:01:50.646 --> 00:01:52.230
put 'em in the message boards.

00:01:52.230 --> 00:01:53.400
We'll be able to look at those questions

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:55.034
and I'll try to surface as many as I can

00:01:55.034 --> 00:01:57.130
during our conversation.

00:01:57.130 --> 00:01:59.750
But Wendy, maybe a good place to start.

00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:02.460
I think a lot of, maybe
a lot of folks have heard

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:04.410
for Teach for All, maybe not everyone has.

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:06.200
A lot of folks might have
heard for Teach for America,

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:07.320
some might not have.

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:09.983
What is Teach for All and
what is Teach for America?

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:14.290
- So, I'll start with Teach for All.

00:02:14.290 --> 00:02:18.413
Teach for All started
just about 14 years ago.

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:22.300
And it was, I mean, I had my head down,

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:24.020
and I'll get back to this in a minute,

00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:26.830
just working to grow the
impact of Teach for America,

00:02:26.830 --> 00:02:30.003
which I had started
maybe 30 plus years ago.

00:02:30.890 --> 00:02:33.900
And I wasn't thinking at all
about the rest of the world,

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:35.740
but there was just something in the water.

00:02:35.740 --> 00:02:38.970
And I started meeting amazing people,

00:02:38.970 --> 00:02:42.130
social entrepreneurs
from all over the world,

00:02:42.130 --> 00:02:44.690
who were interested in seeing
something similar happen

00:02:44.690 --> 00:02:45.980
in their countries.

00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:49.310
And so, they were looking
for support and help

00:02:49.310 --> 00:02:51.790
and that led to the
launch of Teach for All

00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:55.950
as a network of independent,
locally led organizations

00:02:55.950 --> 00:02:58.700
now in 60 countries around the world

00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:00.740
that all share a common purpose,

00:03:00.740 --> 00:03:03.020
which is to develop collective leadership

00:03:03.020 --> 00:03:05.720
to ensure all kids
fulfill their potential.

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:10.240
And we all share a common
approach for doing that,

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:13.170
which is really kind of the approach that

00:03:13.170 --> 00:03:15.480
we pioneered initially
at Teach for America

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:18.400
of enlisting promising leaders,

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:21.130
people of all different
academic backgrounds

00:03:21.130 --> 00:03:24.230
and career interests, and
committing at least two years

00:03:24.230 --> 00:03:26.450
to teach in under-resourced communities,

00:03:26.450 --> 00:03:28.110
investing in their leadership,

00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:31.380
supporting them as they
commit really their lives

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:33.760
to working as leaders and advocates

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:37.360
for the systemic changes
necessary to create

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:42.360
a fair and equitable and
excellent education system.

00:03:43.730 --> 00:03:47.392
- There's so much in
there I hope to unpack.

00:03:47.392 --> 00:03:50.840
You just explained a little bit about

00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:54.230
how Teach for All came to
be and its theory of action.

00:03:54.230 --> 00:03:56.160
And it really did, to some degree,

00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:57.790
emerge out of the work
that you've been doing

00:03:57.790 --> 00:04:00.350
for many decades at Teach for America.

00:04:00.350 --> 00:04:01.780
Tell us a little bit about that journey

00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:05.460
because you were quite
young when you started

00:04:05.460 --> 00:04:08.023
and I know there's a lot
of young people listening

00:04:08.023 --> 00:04:09.220
who for the most part,

00:04:09.220 --> 00:04:10.930
everyone wants to find
purpose in their life,

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:13.580
everyone wants to know how
do I provide for my family,

00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:15.260
but even more important potentially

00:04:15.260 --> 00:04:18.270
make a dent in the
universe make a difference.

00:04:18.270 --> 00:04:21.560
How did you find your purpose
through Teach for America

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:22.710
and then Teach for all?

00:04:24.260 --> 00:04:25.770
- It's so interesting, Sal

00:04:25.770 --> 00:04:30.140
because this started when
I was a college senior

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:34.050
and I was in a funk for the
first time in my life, really.

00:04:34.050 --> 00:04:36.320
I mean, I had been completely driven,

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:38.560
taking on one issue or another,

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:40.580
and then I got to my senior year

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:42.710
and I realized I have to
figure out what I'm gonna do

00:04:42.710 --> 00:04:43.920
for the rest of my life.

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:47.170
And our generation was
called the Me Generation.

00:04:47.170 --> 00:04:49.190
Supposedly we all just wanted to go out

00:04:49.190 --> 00:04:52.860
and work for banks and
management consulting firms.

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:55.200
And I just, I didn't want to do that.

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:58.550
And I just found myself
just searching for a way

00:04:58.550 --> 00:05:00.460
to assume a big responsibility

00:05:00.460 --> 00:05:02.650
and make a real difference in the world

00:05:02.650 --> 00:05:04.730
and I got this sense that I wasn't alone,

00:05:04.730 --> 00:05:06.770
that there were thousands
of other people out there

00:05:06.770 --> 00:05:08.840
searching for exactly the same thing.

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:10.620
And as I went through all of that,

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:13.870
I'd also become just a very
concerned college student

00:05:13.870 --> 00:05:17.310
focused on the inequities in our country

00:05:17.310 --> 00:05:20.270
and particularly on the
issues in education.

00:05:20.270 --> 00:05:21.810
The fact that where kids are born

00:05:21.810 --> 00:05:25.000
does so much to predict
educational outcomes

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:28.280
and life outcomes and one day
all that just came together.

00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:31.600
And I thought, why
aren't we being recruited

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:35.310
as aggressively to commit
just two years to teach

00:05:35.310 --> 00:05:37.670
in urban and rural public
schools in our country,

00:05:37.670 --> 00:05:39.830
as we were being recruited
to commit just two years

00:05:39.830 --> 00:05:40.810
to work on Wall Street?

00:05:40.810 --> 00:05:43.780
And I just became completely
obsessed with this idea.

00:05:43.780 --> 00:05:45.377
I thought it was gonna change the country.

00:05:45.377 --> 00:05:48.780
It was gonna channel all this
energy in the classrooms,

00:05:48.780 --> 00:05:49.980
which was gonna be really important

00:05:49.980 --> 00:05:51.250
for kids growing up today,

00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:54.660
but I also thought how
different would the country be

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:57.510
if we took all these
quote, promising leaders,

00:05:57.510 --> 00:05:59.480
and have their first
two years out of college

00:05:59.480 --> 00:06:01.630
be teaching in low-income communities

00:06:01.630 --> 00:06:02.820
instead of working at banks.

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:04.680
I thought that would
change our priorities,

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:06.140
our consciousness as a country.

00:06:06.140 --> 00:06:08.360
So that was the big idea.

00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:09.960
I needed a thesis topic.

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:13.093
It was an idea that was
very quickly far beyond me.

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:17.123
Yeah, that's the
beginning of it, at least.

00:06:17.990 --> 00:06:21.920
- Well, I think to some
degree it has changed America.

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:23.450
I think many of those people

00:06:23.450 --> 00:06:25.830
might have gone into banking or something.

00:06:25.830 --> 00:06:27.220
Not that there's anything
wrong with that too.

00:06:27.220 --> 00:06:29.080
There's many people who
do very powerful things

00:06:29.080 --> 00:06:31.310
and very important things after that.

00:06:31.310 --> 00:06:34.030
But I do think, the
number of people I know

00:06:34.030 --> 00:06:35.610
who are Teach for America alumni,

00:06:35.610 --> 00:06:37.080
who know something about education,

00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:38.390
who care about the issue,

00:06:38.390 --> 00:06:40.010
and some of them that stayed in education,

00:06:40.010 --> 00:06:42.160
some of them have gone
into other professions,

00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:44.450
but I think it has changed them

00:06:44.450 --> 00:06:46.930
and it has changed how they lead.

00:06:46.930 --> 00:06:48.470
It has changed how they,

00:06:48.470 --> 00:06:50.440
how their organizations that they now lead

00:06:50.440 --> 00:06:52.090
think about education.

00:06:52.090 --> 00:06:55.740
But I want to go back to
that moment because I, too,

00:06:55.740 --> 00:06:57.880
as a senior and I think
senior year, I was probably,

00:06:57.880 --> 00:07:00.080
we were probably similar in certain ways.

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:02.150
I felt like I had all this energy.

00:07:02.150 --> 00:07:04.440
I felt like I had all of
the stuff for the world,

00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:05.273
but I was like,

00:07:05.273 --> 00:07:09.400
where do I get the maximum
impact on the world?

00:07:09.400 --> 00:07:12.450
And I did also sometimes have big dreams,

00:07:12.450 --> 00:07:14.720
but then I was like, well,
how do I make that happen?

00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:17.240
So what did you do that
allowed you to take

00:07:17.240 --> 00:07:19.290
that pretty bold vision

00:07:19.290 --> 00:07:21.240
and turn it into actual action

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:23.440
that eventually did get pretty close

00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:25.540
or even better than that bold vision?

00:07:25.540 --> 00:07:26.740
'Cause eventually you went global.

00:07:26.740 --> 00:07:28.320
That wasn't even part
of your original vision.

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:29.153
- Yeah.

00:07:30.200 --> 00:07:33.240
So I think I had a gift in that

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:34.980
I had a thesis requirement.

00:07:34.980 --> 00:07:38.180
I mean, I had to write
an undergraduate thesis

00:07:38.180 --> 00:07:40.670
and I was also really
searching for a topic

00:07:40.670 --> 00:07:43.150
and I decided this would be the topic.

00:07:43.150 --> 00:07:48.150
And that really forced me
to think a lot about this.

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:51.610
I mean, I researched the policy context,

00:07:51.610 --> 00:07:54.580
the previous models that
we could learn from,

00:07:54.580 --> 00:07:55.490
like the Peace Corps

00:07:55.490 --> 00:07:58.070
or there was a Federal Teacher
Corps actually in the 60s,

00:07:58.070 --> 00:08:00.103
or Vista in the US.

00:08:00.940 --> 00:08:04.010
I looked at the mood on college campuses.

00:08:04.010 --> 00:08:07.250
And honestly, I spent a
lot of the thesis research

00:08:07.250 --> 00:08:08.110
looking for this.

00:08:08.110 --> 00:08:10.380
I was just, the more I got into it,

00:08:10.380 --> 00:08:13.370
the more it just was so
clear the timing was perfect

00:08:13.370 --> 00:08:15.450
and this must be happening.

00:08:15.450 --> 00:08:17.400
I was looking, who's doing this?

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:19.060
Because at the time, first of all,

00:08:19.060 --> 00:08:20.720
there were huge teacher shortages.

00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:21.720
I mean, huge headlines,

00:08:21.720 --> 00:08:23.650
like 1,200 teachers short in New York,

00:08:23.650 --> 00:08:25.613
1,000 teachers short in LA.

00:08:26.550 --> 00:08:30.190
There was also a real interest
in the country for the first.

00:08:30.190 --> 00:08:32.950
Fortune magazine cover stories on how

00:08:32.950 --> 00:08:35.250
corporate America was
gonna take on education.

00:08:36.450 --> 00:08:38.170
And I just thought...

00:08:38.170 --> 00:08:40.420
Yeah anyway, that became
how I did it, honestly.

00:08:40.420 --> 00:08:42.310
Like I ended up becoming,

00:08:42.310 --> 00:08:44.190
I realized no one's doing it

00:08:44.190 --> 00:08:45.720
and the time is gonna pass.

00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:47.220
Like, someone has to do it.

00:08:47.220 --> 00:08:49.230
And so I developed a four page plan

00:08:49.230 --> 00:08:50.700
saying this is how we're gonna do it.

00:08:50.700 --> 00:08:54.290
And turned in the thesis,
boil it down into 30 pages,

00:08:54.290 --> 00:08:55.820
and I sent it to all those executives

00:08:55.820 --> 00:08:57.753
quoted in that magazine article.

00:08:59.897 --> 00:09:00.730
There was no email.

00:09:00.730 --> 00:09:02.957
I was mailing letters,
calling, following up,

00:09:02.957 --> 00:09:06.660
and I got a few meetings
off of that, those letters.

00:09:06.660 --> 00:09:10.070
And again, it was one of those ideas

00:09:10.070 --> 00:09:11.177
that was capturing people.

00:09:11.177 --> 00:09:12.730
And I got a seed grant.

00:09:12.730 --> 00:09:15.630
I spent my summer just
trying to meet anyone

00:09:15.630 --> 00:09:16.463
who would meet me.

00:09:16.463 --> 00:09:18.030
Of course, I had no credibility.

00:09:18.030 --> 00:09:21.450
I mean, I would send 100 letters
and get a meeting or two.

00:09:21.450 --> 00:09:26.450
But as I tell everyone,
you can get 98 no's or 99,

00:09:26.830 --> 00:09:28.560
if you get one person saying yes,

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:32.360
and that sort of became
how like ultimately,

00:09:32.360 --> 00:09:33.890
we gained a little traction.

00:09:33.890 --> 00:09:36.230
But honestly, one more thing to share.

00:09:36.230 --> 00:09:37.500
Everywhere I went, I mean,

00:09:37.500 --> 00:09:40.110
I was getting into superintendents

00:09:40.110 --> 00:09:43.870
and potential donors, and various people.

00:09:43.870 --> 00:09:45.590
Again, I would meet one person

00:09:45.590 --> 00:09:47.390
and they would lead me to others.

00:09:47.390 --> 00:09:50.850
Everywhere I went, people
said this is a great idea.

00:09:50.850 --> 00:09:52.320
It will never work.

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:53.950
And the reason they
thought it wouldn't work

00:09:53.950 --> 00:09:56.340
is they thought the college
students would never do it.

00:09:56.340 --> 00:09:59.640
I had a list of 100
campuses, public and private,

00:09:59.640 --> 00:10:02.220
historically black colleges
and Ivy League schools.

00:10:02.220 --> 00:10:03.620
And people looked at the list and said

00:10:03.620 --> 00:10:05.620
you'll never get kids from these schools

00:10:05.620 --> 00:10:08.000
to want to teach in
low-income communities.

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:10.970
And that was the one thing I had a reason

00:10:10.970 --> 00:10:12.460
to have conviction about.

00:10:12.460 --> 00:10:13.690
And so that became the plan.

00:10:13.690 --> 00:10:15.080
We're gonna show people.

00:10:15.080 --> 00:10:18.750
So, people were walking through the door,

00:10:18.750 --> 00:10:19.740
other recent grads.

00:10:19.740 --> 00:10:22.190
I mean, we'd launched a
grassroots recruitment campaign,

00:10:22.190 --> 00:10:24.543
flyers under doors, again, no email.

00:10:25.600 --> 00:10:28.200
And, you know, we had 2,500 people apply

00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:31.670
within three or four
months that generated media

00:10:33.900 --> 00:10:38.420
after I, yeah, I mean, one
year after I graduated,

00:10:38.420 --> 00:10:39.310
it had all come together.

00:10:39.310 --> 00:10:42.420
We had 500 people in
an auditorium embarking

00:10:42.420 --> 00:10:44.570
on a summer training Institute.

00:10:44.570 --> 00:10:46.427
We had had experienced teachers
from all over the country

00:10:46.427 --> 00:10:48.300
and urban and rural areas come together

00:10:48.300 --> 00:10:50.290
to be trainers for them.

00:10:50.290 --> 00:10:52.980
We had school systems in six
places agree to hire them.

00:10:52.980 --> 00:10:54.950
And somehow we raised two
and a half million dollars

00:10:54.950 --> 00:10:55.783
to fund it.

00:10:55.783 --> 00:10:56.790
Anyway, that was the first year.

00:10:56.790 --> 00:10:58.700
But I always tell people
that's when the learning curve

00:10:58.700 --> 00:11:00.900
began because these folks started teaching

00:11:00.900 --> 00:11:03.310
and we realized how hard this would be.

00:11:03.310 --> 00:11:05.780
And that's what's kept me in it.

00:11:05.780 --> 00:11:06.613
You know,

00:11:06.613 --> 00:11:10.140
just the incredible learning
curve and learning journey

00:11:10.140 --> 00:11:13.110
about how to actually do this well,

00:11:13.110 --> 00:11:15.730
like how to recruit and select
people who are ready for this

00:11:15.730 --> 00:11:18.630
and train and support them
to succeed with their kids,

00:11:18.630 --> 00:11:20.020
to learn the right lessons,

00:11:20.020 --> 00:11:22.960
to ultimately fulfill
their potential as a force

00:11:22.960 --> 00:11:25.023
for the systemic changes that we need.

00:11:26.141 --> 00:11:26.974
Yeah.

00:11:27.816 --> 00:11:28.897
- I want to get into that in a second,

00:11:28.897 --> 00:11:31.250
but I also want to make sure
we don't glaze over some things

00:11:31.250 --> 00:11:32.950
that I think you just glazed over.

00:11:34.460 --> 00:11:37.040
You have this thesis and that
does seem like a powerful,

00:11:37.040 --> 00:11:39.170
forcing function to doing your research,

00:11:39.170 --> 00:11:41.680
thinking through the plan,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:11:41.680 --> 00:11:45.980
But then I am really fascinated
by that moment from idea

00:11:45.980 --> 00:11:48.180
to actually starting to get
a little bit of traction.

00:11:48.180 --> 00:11:51.850
So you were literally took
copies of essentially your thesis

00:11:51.850 --> 00:11:53.200
or version of your thesis.

00:11:53.200 --> 00:11:56.540
You put 'em in like manila
envelopes, figure it out.

00:11:56.540 --> 00:12:00.200
Somehow the mailing address,
like you just say, like,

00:12:00.200 --> 00:12:01.850
you know, big corporation X,

00:12:01.850 --> 00:12:03.450
which you could find the address
and you just put the name

00:12:03.450 --> 00:12:07.463
of the CEO on top and hoping,
and it did actually work.

00:12:09.210 --> 00:12:11.770
- Okay, so I had a mix of people.

00:12:11.770 --> 00:12:14.810
I took the names in the article quoted

00:12:14.810 --> 00:12:16.870
about how we needed like
the head of the business

00:12:16.870 --> 00:12:19.340
round table at the time
or the next person.

00:12:19.340 --> 00:12:21.290
And yeah, I looked up their addresses

00:12:21.290 --> 00:12:25.070
and mailed them letters and
then I would call and follow up.

00:12:25.070 --> 00:12:27.540
And I also added some other CEOs

00:12:27.540 --> 00:12:29.330
from random companies out of like

00:12:29.330 --> 00:12:31.193
the book of big corporation.

00:12:32.452 --> 00:12:35.570
I mean, it, honestly, I had
the advantage of naivete

00:12:35.570 --> 00:12:39.540
and I had been, I mean,
so I got seven meetings

00:12:39.540 --> 00:12:40.410
off those letters.

00:12:40.410 --> 00:12:42.215
That's one thing to note, I sent 30.

00:12:42.215 --> 00:12:43.048
- That's pretty good.

00:12:43.048 --> 00:12:43.881
- I got seven meetings,

00:12:43.881 --> 00:12:45.630
not necessarily with the CEOs, by the way,

00:12:45.630 --> 00:12:47.280
but whoever they handed them down to,

00:12:47.280 --> 00:12:50.520
and one of those meetings, someone said,

00:12:50.520 --> 00:12:52.700
you can use our office in Manhattan.

00:12:52.700 --> 00:12:56.050
So I got some office
space and the other said,

00:12:56.050 --> 00:12:59.650
it was just taken with the
idea and it's a long story,

00:12:59.650 --> 00:13:00.820
but he said,

00:13:00.820 --> 00:13:04.170
if you can get Princeton
to be the conduit of funds,

00:13:04.170 --> 00:13:05.800
I'll make the seed grant.

00:13:05.800 --> 00:13:09.430
And I had met the Princeton
Development Director

00:13:09.430 --> 00:13:11.660
because my thesis advisor
had insisted on it.

00:13:11.660 --> 00:13:13.560
This man was gonna explain
to me how impossible

00:13:13.560 --> 00:13:15.040
this was gonna be.

00:13:15.040 --> 00:13:16.710
And I called that man.

00:13:16.710 --> 00:13:19.200
And he was like, I cannot
believe you got a grant.

00:13:19.200 --> 00:13:20.580
And he made it happen.

00:13:20.580 --> 00:13:21.780
So anyway.

00:13:21.780 --> 00:13:24.543
- And this I'm just hearing,
how much was the seed grant?

00:13:26.149 --> 00:13:28.299
- $26,000, I mean, there's

00:13:28.299 --> 00:13:30.347
- $26,000!

00:13:30.347 --> 00:13:32.230
- I said, I'll come up with a budget.

00:13:32.230 --> 00:13:34.470
These were my expenses for
the summer to fly all over.

00:13:34.470 --> 00:13:36.790
- In today's money, that might be $40,000,

00:13:36.790 --> 00:13:39.250
but not like an enormous amount of money,

00:13:39.250 --> 00:13:41.920
but this was enough
money, was this, you know,

00:13:41.920 --> 00:13:42.753
you was it like,

00:13:42.753 --> 00:13:45.150
you could start taking a salary

00:13:45.150 --> 00:13:47.130
to start flushing it out more.

00:13:47.130 --> 00:13:49.570
You could start what other
other costs you might do

00:13:49.570 --> 00:13:50.640
to do research.

00:13:50.640 --> 00:13:52.610
- I don't even know if I had
a salary, but I had no money.

00:13:52.610 --> 00:13:53.600
So I needed to live.

00:13:53.600 --> 00:13:56.760
So it was like expenses
for a summer of like,

00:13:56.760 --> 00:13:58.257
exploring whether this could work.

00:13:58.257 --> 00:14:00.590
That that was how I
framed the whole thing.

00:14:00.590 --> 00:14:02.060
- And how did you go from that?

00:14:02.060 --> 00:14:04.880
And I do want to go into all
of the learning over the last,

00:14:04.880 --> 00:14:06.310
especially well, recent times,

00:14:06.310 --> 00:14:08.087
but especially over
the last three decades,

00:14:08.087 --> 00:14:11.420
but how did you go from
that to a year later

00:14:11.420 --> 00:14:13.030
having 500 people in a room

00:14:13.030 --> 00:14:14.263
and having all of these,

00:14:15.216 --> 00:14:17.710
it's one thing to get a $26,000 grant.

00:14:17.710 --> 00:14:21.330
It's another thing to actually,

00:14:21.330 --> 00:14:22.780
even though they were skeptical, I,

00:14:22.780 --> 00:14:25.060
in hindsight it feels like
the student part have been the

00:14:25.060 --> 00:14:27.720
easier part, but getting
the districts to say, yes,

00:14:27.720 --> 00:14:29.970
we will place these teachers,

00:14:29.970 --> 00:14:32.240
having enough money to do the training

00:14:32.240 --> 00:14:34.100
for all of these folks,

00:14:34.100 --> 00:14:36.610
being able to do it in a
responsible way so that you're

00:14:36.610 --> 00:14:37.670
gonna put them in classrooms,

00:14:37.670 --> 00:14:40.590
they're gonna be teaching
kids, et cetera, et cetera.

00:14:40.590 --> 00:14:44.270
Like, was it a, just a
series of small steps?

00:14:44.270 --> 00:14:48.840
- Yeah, I mean, it was all in
the four-page plan, but yes.

00:14:48.840 --> 00:14:53.840
I will say, I mean, I think
every piece of this work is,

00:14:54.040 --> 00:14:55.130
is a challenge, right?

00:14:55.130 --> 00:14:58.620
Like I think actually one of
the hardest things we do still

00:14:58.620 --> 00:15:03.420
to this day is inspire people
with a lot of other options

00:15:03.420 --> 00:15:05.050
to say, I want to do this.

00:15:05.050 --> 00:15:08.260
And so, but it is true that,

00:15:08.260 --> 00:15:10.180
that there's a certain
group of people out there.

00:15:10.180 --> 00:15:11.310
I think in that first year,

00:15:11.310 --> 00:15:14.600
we magnetized the people who
saw this and were, you know,

00:15:14.600 --> 00:15:15.800
I want to be part of that.

00:15:15.800 --> 00:15:20.270
And I think as we work to
grow beyond that core group,

00:15:20.270 --> 00:15:22.280
it became all the more
challenging to figure out

00:15:22.280 --> 00:15:23.113
how to do it.

00:15:24.367 --> 00:15:28.210
You know, the school district
part was not the hard part.

00:15:28.210 --> 00:15:30.750
I still remember these
initial meetings I had

00:15:30.750 --> 00:15:32.920
with urban district superintendents

00:15:32.920 --> 00:15:34.030
and heads of human resources,

00:15:34.030 --> 00:15:36.080
like one very memorable meeting

00:15:36.080 --> 00:15:37.850
with the head of human resources

00:15:37.850 --> 00:15:39.860
in the Los Angeles school district.

00:15:39.860 --> 00:15:41.640
The guy started laughing.

00:15:41.640 --> 00:15:44.380
He was looking at my list
of a hundred colleges

00:15:44.380 --> 00:15:45.557
and he just laughed and said,

00:15:45.557 --> 00:15:48.017
"I'll tell you what, you get students

00:15:48.017 --> 00:15:50.827
"from these colleges to
want to teach in my schools.

00:15:50.827 --> 00:15:52.660
"And I will hire all 500 of them."

00:15:52.660 --> 00:15:54.133
And that was kind of the
reaction I was getting,

00:15:54.133 --> 00:15:56.700
because there really there was just,

00:15:56.700 --> 00:15:58.710
there was such a need for teachers.

00:15:58.710 --> 00:16:00.660
And there was, in many ways,

00:16:00.660 --> 00:16:03.120
it was a very different
world 30 years ago.

00:16:03.120 --> 00:16:05.170
I mean, we've seen school
districts in the U S

00:16:05.170 --> 00:16:07.180
become so much more sophisticated

00:16:07.180 --> 00:16:09.800
in how they're recruiting teachers.

00:16:09.800 --> 00:16:12.773
I mean, it used to be
a very different world.

00:16:13.720 --> 00:16:14.930
Figuring out how to train them.

00:16:14.930 --> 00:16:18.300
I found my way to really
progressive teacher educators.

00:16:18.300 --> 00:16:20.470
And I knew, I knew we knew nothing.

00:16:20.470 --> 00:16:24.760
So, I mean, we kind of
subcontracted with them and said,

00:16:24.760 --> 00:16:26.800
please design a training
institute, you know?

00:16:26.800 --> 00:16:30.560
And there was a lot, I mean,
you know, we were working 24/7,

00:16:30.560 --> 00:16:32.030
there was a team of people, of course,

00:16:32.030 --> 00:16:34.930
like a bunch of other recent graduates,

00:16:34.930 --> 00:16:37.800
some who had taught some who
had done the Peace Corps,

00:16:37.800 --> 00:16:39.490
others who were straight out of college

00:16:39.490 --> 00:16:41.310
and were inspired by this mission.

00:16:41.310 --> 00:16:44.680
But we put together an
amazing team of people,

00:16:44.680 --> 00:16:48.330
young people who just made
it happen through a lot of

00:16:48.330 --> 00:16:51.393
all-nighters and, you know, campaign.

00:16:53.470 --> 00:16:56.011
- I think, I think young
people who are watching this,

00:16:56.011 --> 00:16:58.990
I'm sure they are likely
impressed because it's not common

00:16:58.990 --> 00:17:00.230
that you'll see a college senior

00:17:00.230 --> 00:17:02.210
or someone who's 21 or 22 years old,

00:17:02.210 --> 00:17:03.440
be able to do this level,

00:17:03.440 --> 00:17:06.690
but we've had the last 20 or
30 years of seeing examples of,

00:17:06.690 --> 00:17:08.880
especially in the for-profit
sector of startups being

00:17:08.880 --> 00:17:11.580
founded by college students
and getting off the ground.

00:17:11.580 --> 00:17:13.760
But when you started this,
this was not a normal thing.

00:17:13.760 --> 00:17:16.340
It was not normal for someone
in their early twenties

00:17:16.340 --> 00:17:19.670
to be able to start an,
an entity of substance.

00:17:19.670 --> 00:17:20.960
But anyway, I know we're short of time,

00:17:20.960 --> 00:17:24.060
but there must have been
something really powerful

00:17:24.060 --> 00:17:26.140
about how you were presenting yourself,

00:17:26.140 --> 00:17:27.650
or maybe you just had the right sponsors,

00:17:27.650 --> 00:17:30.320
the right advocates or combination

00:17:30.320 --> 00:17:31.400
that allowed you to get off the ground.

00:17:31.400 --> 00:17:32.630
'Cause I would argue it's even harder

00:17:32.630 --> 00:17:35.340
to start a not-for-profit of,

00:17:35.340 --> 00:17:39.100
meaning relative to even a for-profit.

00:17:39.100 --> 00:17:41.300
- I was obsessed with this idea.

00:17:41.300 --> 00:17:46.300
And I think that was the thing, honestly,

00:17:46.730 --> 00:17:48.340
more than anything else.

00:17:48.340 --> 00:17:52.970
And it was an idea that
magnetized people immediately,

00:17:52.970 --> 00:17:56.090
I mean, and it really
has a sense then, right.

00:17:56.090 --> 00:17:58.000
And now it's doing that globally.

00:17:58.000 --> 00:18:02.270
And there was a lot that that's
really what it came down to.

00:18:02.270 --> 00:18:05.250
It was much less than, I
don't know that I would have,

00:18:05.250 --> 00:18:07.250
I wasn't trying to think
of something to start.

00:18:07.250 --> 00:18:09.110
I mean, that was like
the last thing I was not

00:18:09.110 --> 00:18:10.100
in that mode at all.

00:18:10.100 --> 00:18:10.933
It was just,

00:18:10.933 --> 00:18:12.120
I became so obsessed with this

00:18:12.120 --> 00:18:13.970
and I think that's what made it work.

00:18:15.160 --> 00:18:18.360
- And now fast forward to today,

00:18:18.360 --> 00:18:19.660
what are your big takeaways?

00:18:19.660 --> 00:18:23.480
You had some hypotheses 30
years ago, or whenever it was,

00:18:23.480 --> 00:18:27.340
which of those came true or
maybe are even better than what

00:18:27.340 --> 00:18:29.580
you expected, which of
those the jury's still out.

00:18:29.580 --> 00:18:30.620
And which of those are you like?

00:18:30.620 --> 00:18:32.410
Well, I'm not sure if that was right,

00:18:32.410 --> 00:18:34.496
but there's this other thing we learned.

00:18:34.496 --> 00:18:35.329
- Mm.

00:18:36.360 --> 00:18:38.900
So what's interesting about this, I mean,

00:18:38.900 --> 00:18:40.793
as I shared earlier,

00:18:41.840 --> 00:18:43.780
part of what I was so obsessed about

00:18:43.780 --> 00:18:46.400
was the long-term impact I
thought this would have, like,

00:18:46.400 --> 00:18:48.660
I thought it would really reshape

00:18:48.660 --> 00:18:50.870
the priorities of a generation.

00:18:50.870 --> 00:18:52.850
And I think I probably didn't understand

00:18:52.850 --> 00:18:55.370
exactly how that piece
was going to play out,

00:18:55.370 --> 00:19:00.370
but I think that piece has
happened in spades, you know,

00:19:01.220 --> 00:19:04.420
and now there's all this research
and growing amounts of it

00:19:04.420 --> 00:19:06.460
that look at what happens to these people

00:19:06.460 --> 00:19:07.500
during the two years.

00:19:07.500 --> 00:19:09.290
And we can look at it not only in the U S

00:19:09.290 --> 00:19:11.350
but now in other countries as well.

00:19:11.350 --> 00:19:16.060
And what's fascinating is that
these effects are so similar

00:19:16.060 --> 00:19:17.643
even from country to country,

00:19:18.570 --> 00:19:21.370
like people through those
two years, like they come in,

00:19:21.370 --> 00:19:22.329
I mean,

00:19:22.329 --> 00:19:23.920
they go through this very
rigorous selection process

00:19:23.920 --> 00:19:25.560
and they come in, you know,

00:19:25.560 --> 00:19:27.330
these studies look at people who are right

00:19:27.330 --> 00:19:30.080
on the admissions line, some
didn't get in and some did.

00:19:30.080 --> 00:19:32.800
And then it looks at what
happens to their mindsets

00:19:32.800 --> 00:19:34.990
and beliefs and
understandings two years later

00:19:34.990 --> 00:19:36.440
and beyond that.

00:19:36.440 --> 00:19:39.620
And so they come in, you know,
the selection model works.

00:19:39.620 --> 00:19:42.260
We select people who
believe in kids and families

00:19:42.260 --> 00:19:43.610
and low-income communities,

00:19:43.610 --> 00:19:46.240
but that conviction deepens like the gap

00:19:46.240 --> 00:19:49.340
in their like level of belief
in the potential of kids

00:19:49.340 --> 00:19:51.570
and families grows like, meaning,

00:19:51.570 --> 00:19:53.830
like they believe more
in their kids at the end,

00:19:53.830 --> 00:19:57.510
they also completely shift their
understanding of the nature

00:19:57.510 --> 00:19:58.860
of the problem and the solutions.

00:19:58.860 --> 00:20:01.710
Like they come in thinking
all around the world,

00:20:01.710 --> 00:20:03.590
like the answer is technical, you know,

00:20:03.590 --> 00:20:06.470
it's funding, number one and
number of technical solutions.

00:20:06.470 --> 00:20:10.240
And they come out thinking
this is a big systemic problem

00:20:10.240 --> 00:20:13.390
that's gonna take a lot of adaptive change

00:20:13.390 --> 00:20:16.083
and a lot of leadership
from a lot of places.

00:20:17.010 --> 00:20:18.530
So they basically come out

00:20:18.530 --> 00:20:20.690
having a bigger sense of possibility,

00:20:20.690 --> 00:20:22.990
knowing that no one silver
bullet is gonna do it.

00:20:22.990 --> 00:20:26.020
Like this answer is much more complex

00:20:26.020 --> 00:20:29.130
and they become all the
more committed to the work.

00:20:29.130 --> 00:20:30.780
You know, they changed
their career trajectory.

00:20:30.780 --> 00:20:32.610
They never leave the
work essentially, I mean,

00:20:32.610 --> 00:20:35.770
you look at the 70,000,
some Teach for America,

00:20:35.770 --> 00:20:39.600
alums and 86% of them are
full-time in the work.

00:20:39.600 --> 00:20:40.980
Like some of them in education,

00:20:40.980 --> 00:20:44.290
some of others taking on the
other aspects of the issues

00:20:44.290 --> 00:20:45.320
in low income communities.

00:20:45.320 --> 00:20:46.620
And we're seeing the same thing globally,

00:20:46.620 --> 00:20:51.180
75% of these alumni all over
the world are still full-time

00:20:51.180 --> 00:20:52.013
in education.

00:20:52.013 --> 00:20:54.572
So that part, like the idea
that this was going to shape

00:20:54.572 --> 00:20:57.050
these people and that it
would end up being not only

00:20:57.050 --> 00:20:59.000
about two years, but about every year

00:20:59.000 --> 00:21:03.130
after the two years like
that we got completely right.

00:21:03.130 --> 00:21:06.600
I don't know if I could have known how

00:21:06.600 --> 00:21:08.710
that would play itself out over time.

00:21:08.710 --> 00:21:12.370
You know, I think, you
know, it's probably made

00:21:12.370 --> 00:21:17.126
a bigger impact in the long
run and in my biased opinion

00:21:17.126 --> 00:21:19.640
than I could have predicted

00:21:19.640 --> 00:21:23.090
because of the nature of the
problem and the solution,

00:21:23.090 --> 00:21:25.330
which we can talk about.

00:21:25.330 --> 00:21:26.800
- And I only go in a second,

00:21:26.800 --> 00:21:28.510
but how do you think it's affected

00:21:28.510 --> 00:21:30.703
the education environment?

00:21:30.703 --> 00:21:32.720
What's that alternative reality

00:21:32.720 --> 00:21:35.180
where instead young Wendy Kopp went

00:21:35.180 --> 00:21:37.000
and got a job as management consultant

00:21:37.000 --> 00:21:38.130
and doesn't start Teach for America.

00:21:38.130 --> 00:21:40.230
What would the world look like there?

00:21:40.230 --> 00:21:42.330
- So this is where I was gonna go.

00:21:42.330 --> 00:21:45.560
So I think what I've learned over time

00:21:45.560 --> 00:21:49.230
and what all of these people
who get into this work learn,

00:21:49.230 --> 00:21:51.270
and what I'm sure you've learned is

00:21:51.270 --> 00:21:53.380
that this really is a
big, complex problem.

00:21:53.380 --> 00:21:55.390
Like there is no one thing

00:21:55.390 --> 00:21:58.400
that's gonna solve this problem,
like more great teachers,

00:21:58.400 --> 00:22:00.820
a different curriculum,
a laptop for every kid,

00:22:00.820 --> 00:22:02.640
a charter school, a different government.

00:22:02.640 --> 00:22:04.050
I mean, there's no one thing.

00:22:04.050 --> 00:22:07.270
It's gonna take so many different things.

00:22:07.270 --> 00:22:10.090
Like it really is just
a very complex problem.

00:22:10.090 --> 00:22:12.310
It's not even all about
schools and school systems.

00:22:12.310 --> 00:22:14.620
It's about, you know, it's just,

00:22:14.620 --> 00:22:16.980
it's about the fact that
a whole segments of kids

00:22:16.980 --> 00:22:19.700
face many extra challenges
that more privileged kids

00:22:19.700 --> 00:22:22.380
don't face, discrimination, poverty,

00:22:22.380 --> 00:22:24.770
lack of access to adequate healthcare

00:22:24.770 --> 00:22:26.330
and so many other things.

00:22:26.330 --> 00:22:28.910
And then they show up
at schools never set up

00:22:28.910 --> 00:22:30.030
to meet their extra needs.

00:22:30.030 --> 00:22:32.910
And there's this kind
of prevailing ideology

00:22:32.910 --> 00:22:36.730
about the potential of these
kind of marginalized kids.

00:22:36.730 --> 00:22:41.570
And so to solve that is gonna
take collective leadership.

00:22:41.570 --> 00:22:45.160
You know, it's gonna take a
lot of people working together.

00:22:45.160 --> 00:22:47.910
It's gonna take teachers
and school leaders

00:22:47.910 --> 00:22:49.180
and school system leaders.

00:22:49.180 --> 00:22:50.880
It's gonna take the social entrepreneurs,

00:22:50.880 --> 00:22:53.310
figuring out how to change the system.

00:22:53.310 --> 00:22:55.450
It's gonna take policy leaders.

00:22:55.450 --> 00:22:58.910
It's gonna take students
and parents and advocates.

00:22:58.910 --> 00:23:01.410
You don't, we need a
whole ecosystem of people

00:23:01.410 --> 00:23:02.480
exerting leadership.

00:23:02.480 --> 00:23:07.480
And so I think what teach for
all network organizations have

00:23:07.780 --> 00:23:11.020
done is contributed to
that collective leadership.

00:23:11.020 --> 00:23:13.970
Like they're enlisting people, you know,

00:23:13.970 --> 00:23:15.470
from very diverse backgrounds,

00:23:15.470 --> 00:23:19.070
some who have lived these
inequities that we're addressing

00:23:19.070 --> 00:23:21.130
and others from much more
privileged backgrounds.

00:23:21.130 --> 00:23:22.960
So diverse group of folks,

00:23:22.960 --> 00:23:25.480
they're immersing them in this experience.

00:23:25.480 --> 00:23:27.410
And those people come out believing

00:23:27.410 --> 00:23:28.860
we can solve this problem,

00:23:28.860 --> 00:23:30.580
understanding the nature of the problem

00:23:30.580 --> 00:23:32.900
and taking it on alongside
and in partnership

00:23:32.900 --> 00:23:36.810
with many others, you
know, the systemic issues.

00:23:36.810 --> 00:23:39.240
And so I think what's changed.

00:23:39.240 --> 00:23:40.610
Like you can see now, I mean,

00:23:40.610 --> 00:23:43.990
in the U S you can see communities
where Teach for America's

00:23:43.990 --> 00:23:47.103
been placing a steady stream
of people for 30 years.

00:23:48.040 --> 00:23:49.660
And I know it doesn't seem like it

00:23:49.660 --> 00:23:52.510
because we still have massive inequities.

00:23:52.510 --> 00:23:54.510
And we're far from where we want to be

00:23:54.510 --> 00:23:57.010
as a country in education.

00:23:57.010 --> 00:24:00.730
But if you actually looked at
where things were 30 years ago

00:24:00.730 --> 00:24:02.700
in these communities
versus where they are now,

00:24:02.700 --> 00:24:04.260
in terms of the state of education,

00:24:04.260 --> 00:24:05.860
the educational outcomes,

00:24:05.860 --> 00:24:07.570
we've moved the needle in a big way.

00:24:07.570 --> 00:24:10.060
And not, of course, just
because of Teach for America.

00:24:10.060 --> 00:24:13.150
But if you took all those
Teach for America people

00:24:13.150 --> 00:24:14.410
out of those communities,

00:24:14.410 --> 00:24:17.370
you would take a lot of
the energy and leadership

00:24:17.370 --> 00:24:20.320
and entrepreneurship that
has fueled and catalyzed,

00:24:20.320 --> 00:24:24.190
and in some cases really
driven the change.

00:24:24.190 --> 00:24:27.980
And we're seeing just the same
effects all around the world.

00:24:27.980 --> 00:24:31.030
It's almost like seeing
the same movie playing

00:24:31.030 --> 00:24:32.970
in different countries.

00:24:32.970 --> 00:24:34.560
- Yeah, and I'll add to that.

00:24:34.560 --> 00:24:36.590
I'm sure it's had impact
on those communities,

00:24:36.590 --> 00:24:39.110
but just even on my own
journey at Khan academy

00:24:39.110 --> 00:24:43.580
over the last 11, 12, 13,
I guess it's been 16 years,

00:24:43.580 --> 00:24:45.200
17 years since I started tutoring family.

00:24:45.200 --> 00:24:47.010
But while I was doing this for real,

00:24:47.010 --> 00:24:48.910
the number of Teach for America folks

00:24:48.910 --> 00:24:51.030
that I've seen leading various orders,

00:24:51.030 --> 00:24:53.730
we've had multiple Teach for
America folks at various stages

00:24:53.730 --> 00:24:56.200
in Khan Academy all the
way to the early days

00:24:56.200 --> 00:24:58.710
who did some of our
school partnership work,

00:24:58.710 --> 00:25:01.500
the number of other
not-for-profits or for-profit,

00:25:01.500 --> 00:25:04.110
but education, ed tech,
whatever you want to call it,

00:25:04.110 --> 00:25:07.640
reform, your alumni are everywhere.

00:25:07.640 --> 00:25:09.390
They are driving these things.

00:25:09.390 --> 00:25:10.980
So I do think there's the direct impact

00:25:10.980 --> 00:25:12.000
on those communities.

00:25:12.000 --> 00:25:14.820
There's the ways that it has
changed these people directly

00:25:14.820 --> 00:25:16.410
and their understanding of the system.

00:25:16.410 --> 00:25:19.280
But it's also these, many of
your folks have gone out there

00:25:19.280 --> 00:25:21.340
and then have become mini Wendy's

00:25:21.340 --> 00:25:23.870
and have started other parts
as well, to your point,

00:25:23.870 --> 00:25:25.230
it's a multidimensional approach.

00:25:25.230 --> 00:25:27.000
There's many vectors here,

00:25:27.000 --> 00:25:29.000
but going into the
teaching itself, you know.

00:25:29.000 --> 00:25:30.610
There's a bunch of
questions that have coming

00:25:30.610 --> 00:25:31.990
in from YouTube.

00:25:31.990 --> 00:25:34.097
Raymond Cronin says, "How
do you get students on board

00:25:34.097 --> 00:25:36.977
"with core classes they're
not interested in?"

00:25:37.960 --> 00:25:38.793
There was another question.

00:25:38.793 --> 00:25:39.727
"Can you describe the progress

00:25:39.727 --> 00:25:42.400
"in education you've seen made?"

00:25:42.400 --> 00:25:45.010
And so taking all that
into account, you know,

00:25:45.010 --> 00:25:48.040
what have you found makes
someone a successful teacher?

00:25:48.040 --> 00:25:49.370
And as to Raymond's question,

00:25:49.370 --> 00:25:50.960
it's not just delivery of content.

00:25:50.960 --> 00:25:53.050
It is motivation of content.

00:25:53.050 --> 00:25:54.290
It's other things.

00:25:54.290 --> 00:25:55.800
What's your takeaways here?

00:25:55.800 --> 00:25:58.053
How do you see that talent
and how do you develop it?

00:25:59.390 --> 00:26:01.960
- So, first of all, on
the teaching question,

00:26:01.960 --> 00:26:03.613
and then let's come back
to that second question,

00:26:03.613 --> 00:26:05.680
because that's a slightly different one,

00:26:05.680 --> 00:26:08.120
but we've learned so much

00:26:08.120 --> 00:26:10.710
and it's an ongoing learning journey.

00:26:10.710 --> 00:26:13.820
I think, you know, my
biggest thought in listening

00:26:13.820 --> 00:26:16.470
to that question and just
thinking about what we've learned

00:26:16.470 --> 00:26:19.840
is that, you know, I guess I've rethought

00:26:19.840 --> 00:26:21.390
what we're even working towards.

00:26:21.390 --> 00:26:25.436
You know, when we started
out in this, I mean,

00:26:25.436 --> 00:26:27.000
of course it's still, I mean,

00:26:27.000 --> 00:26:30.230
we're so driven by equity
across the teach for all network

00:26:30.230 --> 00:26:32.810
and working with the
most marginalized kids.

00:26:32.810 --> 00:26:35.270
I think when I started my
Teach for America journey,

00:26:35.270 --> 00:26:37.290
I was thinking that meant catching kids up

00:26:37.290 --> 00:26:39.190
to where more privileged kids are.

00:26:39.190 --> 00:26:41.100
And progressively,

00:26:41.100 --> 00:26:43.230
we just realized how insufficient that is.

00:26:43.230 --> 00:26:46.520
Like we're catching kids
up in a very broken system.

00:26:46.520 --> 00:26:49.980
When what we should be
doing is enabling kids

00:26:49.980 --> 00:26:53.400
and developing them to
exert the leadership,

00:26:53.400 --> 00:26:55.560
to solve the inequities,

00:26:55.560 --> 00:26:57.880
to shape a better future for themselves,

00:26:57.880 --> 00:26:59.410
like navigate a changing economy.

00:26:59.410 --> 00:27:02.200
And for all of us like solve
these pressing problems

00:27:02.200 --> 00:27:04.643
that face our society.

00:27:05.480 --> 00:27:06.530
So it's interesting,

00:27:06.530 --> 00:27:10.340
like we did a whole set
of studies back in the day

00:27:10.340 --> 00:27:12.560
about what differentiated teachers

00:27:12.560 --> 00:27:15.110
who produced the biggest
gains in student achievement,

00:27:15.110 --> 00:27:16.810
you know, standardized test scores,

00:27:16.810 --> 00:27:19.980
but we've redone all those
studies now looking at what is it

00:27:19.980 --> 00:27:22.620
that differentiates the teachers
who are fostering students'

00:27:22.620 --> 00:27:24.820
agency, their awareness in the world,

00:27:24.820 --> 00:27:27.670
their problem solving
skills, their leadership,

00:27:27.670 --> 00:27:29.660
you know, including their, you know,

00:27:29.660 --> 00:27:32.680
ability to apply the academic
knowledge, to be strong,

00:27:32.680 --> 00:27:35.330
critical thinkers and strong
problem solvers and all.

00:27:36.230 --> 00:27:41.230
And so I guess what's come out
of those studies is the idea

00:27:41.420 --> 00:27:43.850
that the strongest teachers, first of all,

00:27:43.850 --> 00:27:45.310
are so purpose driven.

00:27:45.310 --> 00:27:49.023
Like they are very clear
on their ultimate aim.

00:27:50.180 --> 00:27:54.330
They build really strong
relationships with their students

00:27:54.330 --> 00:27:56.530
and the students' families,

00:27:56.530 --> 00:27:58.783
and other teachers in the school.

00:27:59.750 --> 00:28:01.170
And then they're just,

00:28:01.170 --> 00:28:04.910
they're making choices
that foster student agency

00:28:04.910 --> 00:28:07.973
and awareness and wellbeing and mastery.

00:28:09.900 --> 00:28:10.960
I'm gonna pause.

00:28:10.960 --> 00:28:12.680
I mean, there are so many,

00:28:12.680 --> 00:28:15.063
there's so much to be
said on this topic, but.

00:28:16.100 --> 00:28:18.628
- And what's your sense about, you know,

00:28:18.628 --> 00:28:23.220
when you're looking at
these young people going

00:28:23.220 --> 00:28:26.018
into the Teach for America
or the Teach for All force,

00:28:26.018 --> 00:28:29.360
is there any way to find
that signal early on,

00:28:29.360 --> 00:28:31.110
or do they just have to learn as they go,

00:28:31.110 --> 00:28:34.130
and then how do people change
with time and experience?

00:28:34.130 --> 00:28:35.630
'Cause I've definitely heard
the argument both ways.

00:28:35.630 --> 00:28:37.560
These are incredibly high potential folks,

00:28:37.560 --> 00:28:38.910
but then you might have senior teachers

00:28:38.910 --> 00:28:41.600
who might not have quite
the same background,

00:28:41.600 --> 00:28:43.420
but they've learned things over the years.

00:28:43.420 --> 00:28:45.740
How do you see that develop over time?

00:28:45.740 --> 00:28:46.573
- Yeah.

00:28:48.590 --> 00:28:49.808
I mean,

00:28:49.808 --> 00:28:52.790
every teacher's journey is of
course a little bit different.

00:28:52.790 --> 00:28:55.280
And one thing I would just say, I mean,

00:28:55.280 --> 00:28:57.950
we are trying to, on the teaching side,

00:28:57.950 --> 00:29:02.950
be one more source of, you
know, deeply committed teachers,

00:29:03.280 --> 00:29:07.570
working alongside many other
teachers and allies and parents

00:29:07.570 --> 00:29:10.680
and others trying to help kids, you know,

00:29:10.680 --> 00:29:12.770
meet their extra needs, you know,

00:29:12.770 --> 00:29:15.300
support them to live into
their full potential.

00:29:15.300 --> 00:29:16.150
And it's a journey.

00:29:16.150 --> 00:29:18.250
It's a learning journey
for any new teacher,

00:29:18.250 --> 00:29:21.783
including those who we place in support.

00:29:22.650 --> 00:29:25.310
I'm losing track of your
question, but, yeah.

00:29:25.310 --> 00:29:26.910
- How you think it evolves over time?

00:29:26.910 --> 00:29:28.917
Like what, how do you see the,

00:29:28.917 --> 00:29:31.110
even the, in the two years,

00:29:31.110 --> 00:29:33.590
how do you see your teachers change

00:29:33.590 --> 00:29:34.760
between year one and year two?

00:29:34.760 --> 00:29:36.123
What are they doing differently?

00:29:37.990 --> 00:29:39.375
- Yeah, you know,

00:29:39.375 --> 00:29:42.050
it's so interesting because I just talked

00:29:42.050 --> 00:29:46.860
to a first-year teacher
in Columbia who is working

00:29:46.860 --> 00:29:50.060
with a hundred students
across grades five through 10,

00:29:50.060 --> 00:29:51.750
and he's teaching math and physics,

00:29:51.750 --> 00:29:54.060
and he's never met the kids

00:29:54.060 --> 00:29:57.700
because he's only taught in the pandemic.

00:29:57.700 --> 00:30:00.450
And he's somehow started
by teaching the kids

00:30:00.450 --> 00:30:02.620
about the UN sustainable
development goals.

00:30:02.620 --> 00:30:05.170
And then he got them all involved
in researching the reality

00:30:05.170 --> 00:30:06.910
in rural Colombia.

00:30:06.910 --> 00:30:10.290
And then they became very
concerned about the water shortage

00:30:10.290 --> 00:30:12.020
and the gas shortage.

00:30:12.020 --> 00:30:16.390
And these kids have developed
solutions to those problems.

00:30:16.390 --> 00:30:18.810
And he's literally hooked them
up with professors who are

00:30:18.810 --> 00:30:23.810
like, please walk me through
the 3D thing that you've like.

00:30:23.940 --> 00:30:28.580
So he's a first year teacher
and he immediately just

00:30:28.580 --> 00:30:32.250
oriented towards how do we
foster students' leadership?

00:30:32.250 --> 00:30:33.430
Like, let's do it today.

00:30:33.430 --> 00:30:35.060
'Cause there are lots of
problems in the world today.

00:30:35.060 --> 00:30:38.180
And he, I mean, he said a fifth grade girl

00:30:38.180 --> 00:30:40.060
is leading the team.

00:30:40.060 --> 00:30:42.490
He then entered them into
these different competitions

00:30:42.490 --> 00:30:43.670
and they've done very well.

00:30:43.670 --> 00:30:46.120
And he has a fifth grade
girl leading a team.

00:30:46.120 --> 00:30:49.180
I mean, so it's hard, you know,

00:30:49.180 --> 00:30:51.460
and then there were other
first year teachers who come in

00:30:51.460 --> 00:30:54.770
through our organization who, you know,

00:30:54.770 --> 00:30:59.220
are completely overwhelmed
and who, if they persist,

00:30:59.220 --> 00:31:00.340
you know, by the end of the year,

00:31:00.340 --> 00:31:02.640
wake up and realize they
did make a difference

00:31:02.640 --> 00:31:05.990
and then reflect over
the summer and come in,

00:31:05.990 --> 00:31:07.620
hitting the ground
running the second year.

00:31:07.620 --> 00:31:09.790
I mean, I don't want to
say there's one journey,

00:31:09.790 --> 00:31:13.450
but yeah, it's, I mean,

00:31:13.450 --> 00:31:16.810
we're selecting a group of people who one,

00:31:16.810 --> 00:31:19.980
want to be in this work,
who are deeply driven

00:31:19.980 --> 00:31:23.830
to address these inequities
who want to be in the arena.

00:31:23.830 --> 00:31:27.040
One, you know, have high
expectations for kids and families.

00:31:27.040 --> 00:31:29.400
And we're looking for various things,

00:31:29.400 --> 00:31:33.430
but including a lot of evidence
that they take on big things

00:31:33.430 --> 00:31:35.410
and persevere in the face of challenges,

00:31:35.410 --> 00:31:37.953
because that's a huge part of this.

00:31:38.840 --> 00:31:41.930
We're looking for people who
also build strong relationships

00:31:41.930 --> 00:31:43.550
that can influence and motivate others.

00:31:43.550 --> 00:31:46.590
And I think that's another,
just really fundamental piece.

00:31:46.590 --> 00:31:50.400
I mean, all of our studies
about transformational teachers,

00:31:50.400 --> 00:31:51.233
it's interesting.

00:31:51.233 --> 00:31:53.490
Like the number one
differentiator is that they're

00:31:53.490 --> 00:31:56.340
incredibly relational,
not even just the kids,

00:31:56.340 --> 00:31:59.400
but with anyone like the
other teachers in the building

00:31:59.400 --> 00:32:00.233
and such.

00:32:00.233 --> 00:32:03.600
So, you know, there's a lot to like trying

00:32:03.600 --> 00:32:06.440
to select those folks
and invest putting them

00:32:06.440 --> 00:32:08.850
in a culture and helping them understand

00:32:08.850 --> 00:32:13.230
and see like different models
of teachers who are fostering

00:32:13.230 --> 00:32:15.970
their students' leadership
and building their skills

00:32:15.970 --> 00:32:18.400
and providing the ongoing
coaching and mentoring

00:32:18.400 --> 00:32:22.223
and access to resources
necessary to develop over time.

00:32:23.590 --> 00:32:25.000
- Yeah, makes a ton of sense.

00:32:25.000 --> 00:32:27.590
And I just realized whenever
I'm having a good conversation,

00:32:27.590 --> 00:32:30.040
the time goes far faster than I expect.

00:32:30.040 --> 00:32:30.873
So maybe we finish off this,

00:32:30.873 --> 00:32:34.767
a question from CL Grouse on
Facebook, "Being that we have

00:32:34.767 --> 00:32:36.487
"been a pandemic for almost two years,

00:32:36.487 --> 00:32:38.417
"are there any changes
within the organization

00:32:38.417 --> 00:32:40.350
"that weren't there prior to 2020?"

00:32:40.350 --> 00:32:43.860
So if you could take us home
on what does a pandemic done

00:32:43.860 --> 00:32:46.620
to Teach for All to teaching

00:32:46.620 --> 00:32:49.520
and any other takeaways
you want folks to have?

00:32:49.520 --> 00:32:50.353
- Yeah.

00:32:51.470 --> 00:32:52.303
You know,

00:32:52.303 --> 00:32:55.920
I think this in a way,
Teach for All's network

00:32:55.920 --> 00:32:58.855
was kind of made for this era.

00:32:58.855 --> 00:33:01.740
I think it's just been really affirming

00:33:01.740 --> 00:33:04.690
for the kind of teachers and
leaders we're developing,

00:33:04.690 --> 00:33:07.440
who as the world's schools shut down

00:33:07.440 --> 00:33:11.070
progressively just leaned
in with their own students

00:33:11.070 --> 00:33:13.080
to keep them safe and learning.

00:33:13.080 --> 00:33:14.970
And then we're very quickly enlisted

00:33:14.970 --> 00:33:16.920
by government ministries around the world

00:33:16.920 --> 00:33:18.680
and others to train other teachers

00:33:18.680 --> 00:33:20.100
in distance learning strategies,

00:33:20.100 --> 00:33:21.880
create digital libraries,

00:33:21.880 --> 00:33:24.300
leverage radio and TV
to keep kids learning.

00:33:24.300 --> 00:33:27.280
And so we've just seen so much leadership

00:33:27.280 --> 00:33:29.640
and so much innovation across our network.

00:33:29.640 --> 00:33:33.020
And we've also seen the power
of having a global network

00:33:33.020 --> 00:33:36.890
that would enable all those
teachers and alumni educators

00:33:36.890 --> 00:33:38.320
to learn from each other.

00:33:38.320 --> 00:33:41.523
I mean, we just saw solutions
flying across the world.

00:33:42.520 --> 00:33:45.590
So that's maybe a part of the answer.

00:33:45.590 --> 00:33:47.300
We've been reflecting a lot

00:33:47.300 --> 00:33:50.820
on what we're learning in this era.

00:33:50.820 --> 00:33:54.340
And of course we're seeing
the issues we're addressing,

00:33:54.340 --> 00:33:56.500
with the inequities we're addressing,

00:33:56.500 --> 00:33:59.450
just hugely exacerbated in this time,

00:33:59.450 --> 00:34:03.150
but we're also seeing a
lot of new possibilities,

00:34:03.150 --> 00:34:04.650
like mindset shifts.

00:34:04.650 --> 00:34:07.390
A lot of them among the world's educators.

00:34:07.390 --> 00:34:10.140
I mean our networks educators
and the world's educators

00:34:10.140 --> 00:34:12.680
about the power of leveraging technology,

00:34:12.680 --> 00:34:15.140
the power of engaging students

00:34:15.140 --> 00:34:16.910
in shaping the learning experience,

00:34:16.910 --> 00:34:18.650
like partnering with them to do that.

00:34:18.650 --> 00:34:21.330
The power of partnering
all the more deeply

00:34:21.330 --> 00:34:22.910
with parents,

00:34:22.910 --> 00:34:25.440
the importance of integrating a focus

00:34:25.440 --> 00:34:28.400
on wellbeing and social,
emotional development

00:34:28.400 --> 00:34:31.243
at every single step of
the way and every day

00:34:32.954 --> 00:34:33.870
and probably more.

00:34:33.870 --> 00:34:35.780
But, so we're really thinking about

00:34:35.780 --> 00:34:39.540
how do we sustain those mindsets?

00:34:39.540 --> 00:34:42.840
Because if we can do that,
we really have the potential

00:34:42.840 --> 00:34:46.300
to have a different and
much better school system

00:34:46.300 --> 00:34:47.883
